1. Introduction

The digital transformation of academic institutions has shifted learning, assessment, and student management activities onto online platforms. As a result, universities and colleges generate large volumes of heterogeneous data including structured student records, course documentation, grade reports, and unstructured learning materials such as PDF assignments and lecture files. Institutions require efficient mechanisms to search and retrieve this information.

This project develops a prototype Academic Information Retrieval (IR) System for the fictional SmartEdU College. The system retrieves academic information across structured student data and unstructured PDF documents through a unified search interface. It implements hybrid retrieval combining exact keyword matching and fuzzy search using PostgreSQL trigram similarity. This enables robust handling of typographical errors and imperfect queries, a frequent real-world search challenge.

The system is built using Node.js, Express, Prisma ORM, and PostgreSQL, with PDF text extraction and IR evaluation metrics (precision, recall, F1-score). Results are ranked, merged, deduplicated, and enhanced with context snippets and keyword highlighting for improved usability. This report details the system’s design, implementation, and evaluation, demonstrating alignment with IR principles and coursework requirements.

2. Problem Context and Motivation

SmartEdU currently stores academic information in separate systems. Structured data such as student names, courses, and student numbers are held in databases, whereas academic documents (assignments, reports, lecture notes) exist as PDFs and text files in cloud storage. Staff and instructors often struggle to locate relevant information quickly, especially when files lack consistent naming or metadata.

Traditional file search is insufficient due to:

Multiple formats (structured + unstructured)

Typographical errors in search input

Lack of document text indexing

Absence of search ranking or relevance scoring

No summarisation or contextual previews

This project addresses these issues by implementing a unified IR engine capable of searching both structured and unstructured content, integrating keyword matching with fuzzy retrieval and snippet generation. This aligns with real-world IR challenges discussed by Manning, Raghavan & Schütze (2008) and Baeza-Yates & Ribeiro-Neto (2011).

3. System Architecture

The solution follows a modular architecture using a service-oriented design.

Architecture Layers
Layer	Role
User Interface	Issues search query, renders results with highlights/snippets
Express API	HTTP routing, request validation, timing/logging
Search Service	Executes exact + fuzzy search, merges and ranks results
Database	Stores structured and unstructured data, trigram extension enabled
Evaluation Module	Computes precision, recall, F1 and latency
Search Flow

User enters query q

Express controller calls unifiedSearch(q, mode)

Exact search via Prisma (contains, case-insensitive)

Fuzzy search via PostgreSQL SIMILARITY() > 0.2

Merge exact+fuzzy, remove duplicates

Highlight matched text using <mark>

Build snippets from document rawText

Log query, latency, and result count

Return JSON to UI

