SmartEdU Academic Information Retrieval System

COMP1859 Information Retrieval Coursework – Individual Project

1. Introduction

…
The importance of efficient academic retrieval is well-documented, as modern institutions rely on IR systems to organise heterogeneous information sources and support academic administration (Manning, Raghavan & Schütze, 2008). Integrating structured and unstructured sources reflects the IR pipeline model described by Büttcher, Clarke and Cormack (2016), where data preprocessing, indexing, and ranking ensure search relevance and usability.

2. Problem Context and Motivation

…
Hybrid academic systems increasingly require IR pipelines capable of handling text, metadata, and semistructured sources (Baeza-Yates & Ribeiro-Neto, 2011). The SmartEdU dataset exemplifies such needs: PDF course files lack indexable metadata, leading to search delays and information fragmentation. Research highlights that precision suffers when user queries contain misspellings or partial knowledge (Navarro, 2001), justifying the inclusion of fuzzy retrieval.

3. System Architecture

…
The layered design aligns with modular IR architectures separating query processing, retrieval, and ranking (Büttcher, Clarke & Cormack, 2016). Logging and latency monitoring follow recommended IR evaluation practices for measuring search effectiveness (Manning et al., 2008).

4. Data Sources and Storage Design

Storing extracted text in rawText mirrors the document representation stage seen in modern IR pipelines (Büttcher et al., 2016). This facilitates full-text matching and snippet generation, a feature strongly associated with improved search context comprehension (Manning et al., 2008).

5. Retrieval Methods

…
Substring matches form the baseline method, consistent with Boolean retrieval foundations (Baeza-Yates & Ribeiro-Neto, 2011). Meanwhile, trigram similarity represents one established method in approximate string matching literature, recognised for balancing performance and typographical tolerance (Navarro, 2001).

The hybrid model aligns with retrieval fusion strategies suggested in IR literature to optimise precision–recall trade-offs (Manning et al., 2008).

6. Handling Structured and Unstructured Data

…
Academic IR systems typically employ combined approaches to support structured queries (names, IDs) and free-text search (documents) (Büttcher et al., 2016). Snippet context windows follow usability research findings where contextual previewing improves user decision-making and reduces query reformulation (Manning et al., 2008).

7. System Implementation

…
Highlighting and snippet generation are standard document ranking cues where emphasis and proximity improve perceived search relevance (Baeza-Yates & Ribeiro-Neto, 2011).

8. Evaluation and Results

Evaluation uses precision, recall, and F1-score, core IR metrics introduced by van Rijsbergen (1979) and standard in modern academic IR experimentation (Manning et al., 2008).
Latency measurements reflect efficiency requirements emphasised in search system evaluation studies (Büttcher et al., 2016).

The use of ground-truth relevance judgements follows accepted offline IR evaluation methodologies (Baeza-Yates & Ribeiro-Neto, 2011).

9. Discussion and Reflection

…
Trading precision for recall in fuzzy mode mirrors documented patterns in approximate string matching (Navarro, 2001). The hybrid mode follows known multi-stage retrieval recommendations that combine lexical scoring and relaxed matching (Manning et al., 2008).

10. Limitations and Future Work

The absence of semantic embeddings reflects practical prototype constraints; however, future models may adopt vector-based retrieval (e.g., BERT), an evolution highlighted in contemporary IR studies (Devlin et al., 2019).

11. Conclusion

…
The implementation aligns with best-practice IR system development principles emphasising data preprocessing, retrieval, ranking, and evaluation (Büttcher et al., 2016).

