User Input (Next.js UI)
        │
        ▼
Search Form → /api/search?q=...&mode=...
        │
        ▼
Backend IR Engine (Node + Prisma + PostgreSQL)
        │
        ▼
Receive JSON:
- students[]
- courses[]
- documents[] + snippet + <mark>highlight</mark>
- timeMs
        │
        ▼
UI Components:
- StudentList.jsx
- CourseList.jsx
- DocumentList.jsx



8. Testing & Results

This system was tested through browser requests, Postman, the Next.js UI, and an automated IR evaluation script. The goal was to validate correctness, effectiveness, and performance across both structured and unstructured academic data. Testing covered exact search, fuzzy matching, hybrid retrieval, response time, and edge-case behaviour.

8.1 Functional Testing

The API was run locally at:

http://localhost:5000


The frontend consumed it via:

NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:5000


Test requests confirmed that:

Valid queries returned relevant students, courses, and documents

Queries with misspellings recovered relevant results in fuzzy and hybrid mode

Document results included contextual snippets

<mark> highlighting tags were correctly returned and rendered by the UI

Empty queries returned empty arrays without errors

Search logs captured query text, response time, and result count

8.2 Structured vs. Unstructured Retrieval

Structured queries such as "john" retrieved student records.
Content-based queries such as "assignment" retrieved PDF academic documents and highlighted matched terms inside snippets.
This demonstrates correct integration of both data types.

8.3 Precision, Recall, and F1-Score Evaluation

A ground-truth dataset was created and the automated evaluation script executed each query across three modes:

Exact match

Fuzzy match (pg_trgm)

Hybrid (exact + fuzzy)

Results showed:

Exact search had perfect precision for clean queries but failed on misspellings

Fuzzy mode recovered misspellings but occasionally reduced precision

Hybrid mode consistently provided the highest overall F1-score

Latency increased in fuzzy mode due to similarity scans, as expected

These results align with information retrieval theory regarding precision/recall trade-offs (Manning et al., 2008; Navarro, 2001).

8.4 Performance Testing

Response time was measured via response-time middleware and observation in the UI. The system consistently responded within millisecond ranges for small datasets, with increased latency when fuzzy operators were used. This behavior aligns with IR performance expectations (Büttcher et al., 2016).

8.5 Edge-Case Behavior

Edge cases validated:

Case	Result
Empty query	Empty result set, no error
Misspelled query	Returned matches in fuzzy/hybrid mode
Random string	No matches, safe exit
Special characters	No crash, query sanitized via ORM
Case variation (John vs john)	Same results
8.6 Summary

The system meets coursework testing requirements by demonstrating:

Correct retrieval behaviour

Hybrid IR performance advantages

Robust handling of structured + unstructured data

Good response times

Proper error handling and input validation

